{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794599164,"nonce":"4zgO4Pnt5hHyK/k+8qObiKwlxKM=","title":"A covered window keeps rendering, and a frame can block the main thread in nextDrawable","message":"On macOS a window that is fully covered by another still gets Frame events and still renders. Nothing in blinc_platform_desktop or blinc_app handles winit's Occluded event, so the redraw chain keeps running and every frame acquires a drawable from the window server.\n\nSeen with two copies of flip_window_check (the second opens at the same default position and covers the first). The first received Occluded(true) and kept drawing at 20 to 44 frames a second for the 13 s it was covered, against 55 to 60 before and after, on a window nobody can see. The loop still ran about 25,000 callbacks a second.\n\nThe frame path is also where the loop blocks when the window server is slow. Stack samples (sample, 1 ms) of the main thread taken as a stall began:\n- A run where the window had just become occluded: 709 of 737 samples in -[CAMetalLayer nextDrawable], 691 of them in its usleep poll, with the loop 115 ms inside a Frame handler.\n- A run with a focus change: nearly all main-thread time in CAMetalDrawable creation and release (IOSurfaceSharedEvent init and dealloc, mach_msg to the window server).\nSo a frame's drawable acquire and release are synchronous IPC on the main thread, and a stall there delays every event, wakes included.\n\nMid-run gaps over 100 ms in flip_window_check: 8 in 12 minutes of 30 s runs (114 to 967 ms), none of them a slow phase of the frame (the frames either side cost 0.6 to 3.5 ms). The two caught with the watchdog both had a window state event within 160 ms: Focused(false), and Occluded(true) at 23.4 s.\n\nProposed: handle Occluded in the platform loop. While a window is occluded, drop its Frame events instead of rendering, and on Occluded(false) request a redraw. frame_dirty stays set across the skipped frames, so the first frame after is a paint. WindowEvent in blinc_platform has no Occluded variant, so this can stay inside blinc_platform_desktop without touching the public enum.\n\nNot known: whether this removes the stalls at the transitions (the in-flight frame can still block), or the 0.3 to 1 s ones; those were not reproduced on demand, only the 110 ms ones at a transition. Not checked: Windows, Linux X11, and whether a minimised window or another Space gets Occluded the same way. macOS screen capture of an occluded window would stop updating; check the recorder.\n\nRelated: f87e115, 77fc97d.","files":null}]}